  "transcript": "# Machinery of government changes\n\n## Was abolishing DSIT the right move?\n> Both witnesses say abolishing DSIT was a mistake because it fragments leadership on science and risks deprioritising long-term research.\n\nChair: We’re looking at the Government’s decision to abolish DSIT and spread its work across other Departments. Was DSIT working and was its removal justified?\nSir Paul Nurse: I think it was a mistake. Science sits across everything—health, defence, energy—and having a single Department focused on it helped raise its profile across Government.\nDr Alicia Greated: We saw some benefits from DSIT: it linked research to innovation and trade. Our members welcome closer ties to business, but they fear instability and short-term thinking will undermine basic research and long-range planning.\nGeorge Freeman: You’ve both warned about loss of focus—what practical harm do you see if science becomes a smaller item inside a much larger Department?\nSir Paul Nurse: When science is a small line among many priorities, civil servants and Ministers naturally focus on urgent business issues. Long-term discovery research is easy to lose sight of.\n\n\n## Disruption, costs and civil service morale\n> Reorganisations cause hidden costs and staff loss; witnesses urge stability so relationships and expertise survive.\n\nDaniel Zeichner: How are civil servants reacting—relief or exasperation?\nDr Alicia Greated: Many are frustrated. Repeated reshuffles wear teams down, drive people out and destroy institutional memory; continuity of personnel matters a lot.\nChair: Are these changes expensive?\nDr Alicia Greated: They carry direct costs—previous estimates put typical reorganisation in the tens of millions—and bigger opportunity costs: time, disrupted projects and lost expertise.\nKit Malthouse: Isn’t the timing catastrophic—science is entering a pivotal moment with AI and life sciences?\nSir Paul Nurse: It’s risky, but we shouldn’t despair. Good people can make imperfect structures work, but they need clear backing, resources and time.\n\n\n## AI: who regulates and who builds policy?\n> AI responsibilities are scattered across Cabinet Office, BIST and DCMS—witnesses call for clear leadership, technical capability and a forcing mechanism to join up policy.\n\nChair: AI work sits in different places: growth in BIST, the taskforce and institutes in Cabinet Office, regulation scattered elsewhere. Where should responsibility sit?\nDr Alicia Greated: The centre getting involved is sensible, but the responsibilities are fragmented. The important thing is to pick a coherent home or a clear mechanism to join the dots.\nSir Paul Nurse: AI is part of science; keeping it close to research helps. If AI policy is split, we need a Cabinet-level backstop so Departments don’t talk past each other.\nTom Collins: What would an effective forcing mechanism look like?\nDr Alicia Greated: Either a strong taskforce with technical and analytical staff, or a Cabinet committee that can arbitrate quickly and push Departments to act together.\nSir Paul Nurse: It should be outcome-focused: who decides, and how will we measure fast-moving risks and adoption? That clarity matters more than labels.\n\n\n## Funding, UKRI and national facilities\n> Witnesses warned that funding pressure and shifting oversight risk damaging national facilities and discovery research; they want transparency and long-term budgets.\n\nFreddie van Mierlo: STFC cuts put facilities like Diamond and ISIS at risk. Should Ministers be checking how UKRI spends its money?\nDr Alicia Greated: Facilities are national assets with fixed costs; repeated cuts often translate into job losses and loss of capability. Decisions must be transparent and carefully analysed.\nSir Paul Nurse: Inflation has eroded research budgets. We lack sufficient public R&D funding versus peer countries—losses are not just about priorities but about overall scale.\nMartin Wrigley: Where do you phone when a major facility needs more running money?\nSir Paul Nurse: There’s no single number to call. We need politicians and Treasury friends who understand science to keep long-term running costs funded—buildings need batteries, not just bricks.\n\n\n## What to do next: leadership, procurement and regional strategy\n> Both witnesses urged clearer Cabinet-level science leadership, stable funding, better procurement for UK firms, oversight of national R&D capability, and careful evaluation of reorganisations.\n\nChair: Conclude with practical actions: three things the Secretary of State must do immediately for science.\nDr Alicia Greated: 1) Provide stability and a long-term R&D strategy; 2) oversee UK R&D capability and the funding pressures on universities and facilities; 3) publish a clear international R&D and trade strategy.\nSir Paul Nurse: First, get science visible at Cabinet—either via a Cabinet Committee or a clearer central mechanism; second, protect discovery research while improving how we translate it; third, secure predictable multi-year funding for core facilities.\nGeorge Freeman: On the innovation nation aim: how do we keep firms and investment here instead of losing them overseas?\nDr Alicia Greated: Be clear about UK strengths, align trade and inward investment plans to R&D clusters, and make procurement and early-stage public investment work better for startups.\nSir Paul Nurse: Unlock long-term private investment—pension funds are a potential source—and be ruthless about cutting failing translation projects while funding the ecosystem that creates future firms.\nChair: Any final short recommendations for Parliament and this Committee?\nDr Alicia Greated: Scrutinise the new arrangements, press for transparent UKRI decisions and facility impact assessments, and push for policies that protect capability nationwide.\nSir Paul Nurse: Insist on political prioritisation: ask Ministers for clear accountability for science and make sure the Treasury and Prime Minister understand the return on investing in research.\n",
